#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
Hollywood Tower Hotel (The Twilight Zone Tower of Terror, Disney's Hollywood
Studios, Walt Disney World, Orlando) -- parametric massing model for FDM printing.

WHAT THIS IS
------------
An Autodesk Fusion script that builds a single, watertight, print-ready solid
of the Tower of Terror massing, auto-scaled to fit a 280 x 280 x 270 mm bed.

HOW TO RUN IT
-------------
1. Fusion "Scripts and Add-Ins" (Shift+S):
     Fusion wants a .py file inside a folder of the same name, e.g.
         .../API/Scripts/HollywoodTower/HollywoodTower.py
     so copy/rename this file accordingly before adding it. Fusion imports the
     module and calls run(context).

2. Fusion MCP / Claude extension:
     Hand the whole file to the bridge's "run python in Fusion" tool.
     AUTO_RUN below fires run(None) when the module is exec'd rather than
     imported. If your bridge uses a namespace where neither guarded name
     matches, just append an explicit `run(None)`, or set AUTO_RUN = False and
     call run(None) yourself.

Nothing here touches Fusion until run() is called, so the file is safe to
store, diff and review with no Fusion session attached.

ACCURACY -- PLEASE READ
-----------------------
Only one dimension of this building is actually published: the tower is
199 ft tall (Disney deliberately kept it under 200 ft so it would not need
FAA aircraft warning beacons). Everything else here -- footprint, bay
spacing, setback heights, window sizes -- is estimated from photographs and
park plans and is marked "approx" below. This is an honest massing model, not
a survey. It reads correctly from a few feet away on a shelf; it is not a
replica of the as-built structure.

Deliberate simplifications:
  * The real wings meet the tower at a slight angle; here they are orthogonal.
  * Mission-tile roofs are smooth sloped frustums, not tiles -- tile courses
    at this scale would be ~0.3 mm, below nozzle resolution.
  * The blasted upper corner is a hand-tuned rectilinear pattern. Every cut
    opens to the sky, so the damage adds no new overhangs.
  * The sign band is a raised plaque. True-scale lettering would be ~1 mm
    tall, so the optional embossed text is deliberately oversized.

PRINTING NOTES
--------------
  * Orientation: as built. Z up, facade facing -Y. No rotation needed.
  * Supports: only the wing / porte-cochere tile roofs need them, because they
    default to the real ~22 deg pitch (a ~68 deg overhang). Set
    SUPPORT_FREE_ROOFS = True to steepen them to 45 deg and print the whole
    model with no supports at all -- less accurate, but no support scarring on
    the most visible surfaces.
  * Everything else is support-free: the massing only ever steps inward as it
    rises, window recesses are 1.2 mm deep (trivial bridges), the entry arches
    are horizontal-axis circular arcs (short self-supporting bridges at the
    crown), and the storm damage is open above.
  * 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.15-0.2 mm layers. Feature sizes are audited against
    MIN_FEATURE_MM at build time and reported in the log.

Units: Fusion's API works in CENTIMETRES internally. Everything in this file
is authored in feet (real world) or millimetres (model) and converted at the
API boundary by _cm(). Never pass a raw model number to a Fusion call.
"""

import math
import traceback

import adsk.core
import adsk.fusion


